UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 5: Food safety and hygiene', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Cross-contamination is the movement of microbes from a raw food, especially raw meat, chicken or eggs, onto food that will be eaten without further cooking. The usual path is a shared board, knife, plate or drip. The control is separation: different boards, or a full wash between jobs, and raw food stored so it cannot drip downward.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same technology/design idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nRaw mince is on the top fridge shelf above a bowl of cut watermelon. Explain the cross-contamination path and the fix.\n\nFEEDBACK\nJuice can drip onto the watermelon. Move the mince to a covered container on the bottom shelf and keep the fruit above raw meat.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking a quick wipe with a dry paper towel between raw chicken and salad is enough. Washing with detergent is the control.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlan a two-board kitchen for a meal of grilled sausages and a garden salad. Say which board touches which food and what happens to the sausage tongs.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation, criterion or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Food Technology","learning_intention":"Cross-contamination is the movement of microbes from a raw food, especially raw meat, chicken or eggs, onto food that will be eaten without further cooking. The usual path is a shared board, knife, plate or drip. The control is separation: different boards, or a full wash between jobs, and raw food stored so it cannot drip downward.","lesson_number":5,"lesson_role":"Learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe classroom tools/materials under teacher supervision","Device only when the lesson requires digital work"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["FT5-SAF-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Plan a two-board kitchen for a meal of grilled sausages and a garden salad. Say which board touches which food and what happens to the sausage tongs. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using cross-contamination on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking a quick wipe with a dry paper towel between raw chicken and salad is enough.. Correction: Juice can drip onto the watermelon.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking a quick wipe with a dry paper towel between raw chicken and salad is enough. Washing with detergent is the control. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating cross-contamination on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with cross-contamination, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about cross-contamination with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for cross-contamination. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":