record a test result, and note one honest improvement"],"question":"Which action keeps design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence accurate for cross-contamination?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example","Thinking a quick wipe with a dry paper towel between raw chicken and salad is enough","Name raw only, with no linked example","Plan a two-board kitchen for a meal of grilled sausages and a garden salad"],"question":"Which independent response best applies cross-contamination to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about cross-contamination with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating cross-contamination on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using cross-contamination on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for cross-contamination. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with cross-contamination, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking a quick wipe with a dry paper towel between raw chicken and salad is enough.. Correction: Juice can drip onto the watermelon.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-food-technology-y9-w03-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Year 7 alignment. Food Technology 7–10 (2025) says Stage 4 teachers may adjust Stage 5 outcomes (FT4-ADJ-01). This lesson uses FT5-SAF-01 as a pending proposal. The 2019 syllabus remains in teach-out. Not NESA verified. Practical cooking is optional and adult-supervised.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise cross-contamination.","Read the mission before you start observing, designing or testing.","Keep the property, criterion or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation, criterion or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations, criteria or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation, criterion or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations or criteria.","Say what each observation or criterion shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Food Technology","success_criteria":["I can use cross-contamination with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify raw and explain why it matters.","I can practise ready-to-eat independently on a new prompt.","I can notice separate needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Cross-contamination is the movement of microbes from a raw food, especially raw meat, chicken or eggs, onto food that will be eaten without further cooking.","options":["Cross-contamination is the movement of microbes from a raw food, especially raw meat, chicken or eggs, onto food that","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about cross-contamination?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nJob order on one board: salad first, then raw chicken, then wash the board before anything else. The unsafe order is chicken first and salad second with no wash. The salad is ready-to-eat, so it cannot “cook away” the chicken juice.\n\nTRY FIRST\nRaw mince is on the top fridge shelf above a bowl of cut watermelon. Explain the cross-contamination path and the fix.\n\nFEEDBACK\nJuice can drip onto the watermelon. Move the mince to a covered container on the bottom shelf and keep the fruit above raw meat.","explain":"Cross-contamination is the movement of microbes from a raw food, especially raw meat, chicken or eggs, onto food that will be eaten without further cooking. The usual path is a shared board, knife, plate or drip. The control is separation: different boards, or a full wash between jobs, and raw food stored so it cannot drip downward.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking a quick wipe with a dry paper towel between raw chicken and salad is enough. Washing with detergent is the control. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Prepare salad before raw chicken, or wash the board between them.","caption":"Ready-to-eat food must not follow raw meat on a dirty board.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Unsafe order</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">chicken then salad</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Safer order</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">salad then chicken</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.","who":"pi