and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show technology thinking. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Store raw mince below ready-to-eat food and keep it covered.","caption":"Drip path is cross-contamination.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Top shelf</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">raw mince drips</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Fix</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">mince below, covered</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about cross-contamination?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking a quick wipe with a dry paper towel between raw chicken and salad is enough. Washing with detergent is the control. Here is a more productive model: Job order on one board: salad first, then raw chicken, then wash the board before anything else. The unsafe order is chicken first and salad second with no wash. The salad is ready-to-eat, so it cannot “cook away” the chicken juice.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nJuice can drip onto the watermelon. Move the mince to a covered container on the bottom shelf and keep the fruit above raw meat.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Thinking a quick wipe with a dry paper towel between raw chicken and salad is enough. Washing with detergent is the control. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about cross-contamination?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCross-contamination is the movement of microbes from a raw food, especially raw meat, chicken or eggs, onto food that will be eaten without further cooking. The usual path is a shared board, knife, plate or drip. The control is separation: different boards, or a full wash between jobs, and raw food stored so it cannot drip downward. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same technology idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 5: Food safety and hygiene","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Food safety and hygiene","week":4,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nJob order on one board: salad first, then raw chicken, then wash the board before anything else. The unsafe order is chicken first and salad second with no wash. The salad is ready-to-eat, so it cannot “cook away” the chicken juice.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, criteria, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Prepare salad before raw chicken, or wash the board between them.","caption":"Ready-to-eat food must not follow raw meat on a dirty board.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Unsafe order</text><text